# TODOs

All implementation-strategy phases are complete. Follow-up work should be driven by
new product requirements rather than the retired phase checklist below.

## Testing plan (Completed)

- Python unit and functional suites cover ingestion, persistence, immutable snapshots,
  route contracts, task lifecycles, history isolation, workflows, and the dialect tool.
- Lightweight Node tests cover extracted browser-independent helpers.
- Live LLM traffic remains prohibited in automated tests.

## API and flow refactor (Completed)

- Routers and `/api` compatibility paths are in place; legacy browser routes remain.
- Analysis flows live in `context_doctor.flows` and empty responses propagate visibly.
- Single-rule analysis has an opt-in task-manager endpoint at
  `POST /api/tasks/rule-analysis`; the legacy `/run` browser contract remains sync.

## Context caching (Completed)

- `ContextService` owns validation, normalization, persistence orchestration, explicit
  create/replace, retrieval/touch, deletion, and expiry.
- Cached records include raw and normalized schema, rules, dialect, filenames, identity,
  creation time, and last-used time. History input metadata includes `context_id`.
- Background tasks receive deep immutable snapshots. The UI supports cached identity
  reuse, and `/api/contexts` provides create/replace/get/delete lifecycle endpoints.

## Frontend JavaScript extraction (Completed)

- Package static assets are served by FastAPI; templates contain markup, configuration,
  and data attributes rather than behavior handlers.
- Flow selection, copy/clear, loading, polling, cancellation, partial rendering, and
  paging behavior live in `static/index.js`; pure helpers and Node tests are included.
- The extraction intentionally preserves the visual design and legacy endpoints.

## Agentic tooling decision (Completed)

- The chosen scope is explicit, auditable tools rather than an unconstrained autonomous
  prompt loop. `GET /api/tools/sql-dialect-docs` checks registered official, current
  documentation URLs and returns structured results for future agent orchestration.
- Documentation checks are user-invoked and are never run implicitly during analysis,
  keeping automated tests deterministic and avoiding surprise external traffic.
